    "body": "fix #42598\r\n\r\n## Problem\r\n- `vim.fs.abspath('fugitive:///a')` returns \"<CWD>/fugitive:/a\", so filetype functions get a mangled path for URI buffers.\r\n- `vim.fs.root()` doesn't accept \"file://\" URIs.\r\n- No URI parser, so modules use ad-hoc scheme patterns, e.g. `vim.ui.open('~/a:b')` treats the path as a URL.\r\n\r\n## Solution\r\n- Introduce `vim.uri.parse()`, and use it everywhere.\r\n  - DWIM: a one-letter \"scheme\" is a drive letter.\r\n- `vim.fs.abspath()` returns URLs as-is.\r\n- Expose `vim.uri` a module. Deprecate the old `vim.uri_xx()` names.\r\n\r\nTODO:\r\n\r\n- [x] test coverage for encode/decode\r\n- [x] deprecate() ?\r\n- [x] migrate all internal usages of the old deprecated functions\r\n- [x] introduce `Uri` container object which unambiguously represents a path, reference https://github.com/microsoft/vscode-uri\r\n\r\n\r\n### Design differences vs [vscode Uri](https://github.com/microsoft/vscode-uri) (the magic of \"hindsight\" 😇):\r\n- Decoding:\r\n  - vscode URI.parse() decodes each component, and toString() re-encodes them.\r\n  - Two of its tests are skipped ([#79474](https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/issues/79474), [#45515](https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/issues/45515), [#83645](https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/issues/83645) 💀) because that decode/re-encode loop doesn't return the original string. Our parse() doesn't decode, so it avoids that.\r\n- Missing parts: vscode returns empty string for a missing part, so it can't tell `file:/a` from `file:///a`. We return nil.\r\n- No scheme: in non-strict mode vscode falls back to scheme file; we return nil.\r\n- Drive letters: `URI.parse('C:/a')` gives scheme C, like RFC 3986. vscode deals with drive letters only in URI.file() and fsPath, and lowercases them there.\r\n    - We intentionally deviate from RFC 3986 here, for \"DWIM\" purposes.\r\n- Scheme characters: vscode accepts `_` and a leading digit; we follow RFC 3986.",
    "closed_at": "2026-11-01T18:20:09Z",
